    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §398-f, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S2455\n\nSPONSOR: KRUEGER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general business law, in relation to prohibiting\nconsumer contract provisions waiving the consumer's right to make state-\nments concerning goods or services\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nRelates to prohibiting the consumer contract provisions waiving the\nconsumer's right to make statements concerning goods or services.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 adds section 399-zzzzz to the general business law to prohibit\nconsumers from signing contracts that waive the right of the consumer to\nmake statements regarding the seller or lessor and to create a civil\npenalty to sellers or lessors who impose or propose contracts that waive\nsuch rights of the consumers.\n\nSection 2 states the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn recent years, review websites and applications, like Yelp and TripAd-\nvisor, have rapidly gained popularity with allowing consumers to post\nand comment on reviews of sellers and lessors of goods and services.\nWhen signing up for such websites and applications, consumers agree to\nthe Terms of Services and Privacy Policy.  Currently, websites have the\nability to create contracts in their Terms of Services that require\nconsumers to waive their right to post negative comments about sellers\nor lessors, ultimately infringing on a consumer's right to free speech.\nThis waiver penalizes the consumer for making negative comments about\nsellers and lessors and ultimately allows the seller or lessor to sue\nthe consumer for posting an unfavorable review.\n\nThis bill would protect a consumer's right to make statements regarding\ngoods or services by prohibiting the waiver of these rights. By assign-\ning a civil penalty to review websites and applications that create\nwaivers on a consumer's right to make statements, more consumers will be\ninformed about a consumer's satisfaction with goods and services across\nNew York. This bill does not limit a seller's or lessor's right to a\ncivil course of action for defamation, libel, or slander.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017-18: S.6375 Consumer Protection\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
